@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+---
+status: accepted
+date: 2026-11-11
+decision-makers: master orchestrator (Claude), under the owner's delegation of 2026-11-10; the defect confirmed by the coordinator
+review-by: 2026-12-11
+---
+
+# Cancel-all cancels again an order the node holds as pending cancel
+
+## Context and Problem Statement
+
+ADR 0036's cancel-all skipped an order already `PENDING_CANCEL`, "since a second cancel risks a
+rejection". NautilusTrader puts an order in `PENDING_CANCEL` when the cancel is sent, before Alpaca has
+answered (`execution.py`'s header: NautilusTrader generates `OrderPendingCancel` itself when a command is
+sent). A cancel that got no answer, a read timeout for one (ADR 0039), leaves the order there while it
+still works at Alpaca, and the skip meant no later cancel-all would cancel it. Confirmed as a defect,
+with a test that fails on the skip.
+
+## Decision Drivers
+
+* A cancel-all must not leave a working order uncanceled because of a status the node set itself.
+* No new event an order cannot take: a second cancel must not turn into a false `OrderCancelRejected`.
+
+## Considered Options
+
+1. Keep the skip (ADR 0036; Coinbase's adapter at the pin, as ADR 0036 read it).
+2. Cancel every open order of the command, `PENDING_CANCEL` included, through the single cancel's path.
+3. Ask Alpaca for each pending-cancel order first and cancel only those it still shows open.
+
+## Decision Outcome
+
+Chosen: **option 2**. A pending-cancel order gets one more cancel by ID, the same call as any other. The
+risk ADR 0036 named is already handled on that path: if Alpaca is canceling the order, a second cancel is
+answered 204 and waits for the `canceled` update, or refused 422, and `_cancel_refused` asks Alpaca for
+the order and generates nothing when it shows it canceled or pending cancel (ADR 0036, for alpaca-py's own
+retry). Option 3 adds a lookup to every pending-cancel order for what the 422 path does already. The test
+that showed the defect cites upstream's Kraken spot, Binance spot, Bybit and Interactive Brokers adapters
+as including every open order; that was not re-read for this record (Evidence).
+
+Supersedes, in ADR 0036's "Cancel-all: option 2", the skip of an order already `PENDING_CANCEL`. The rest
+of that decision stands: closed orders are still skipped at their turn.
+
+### Consequences
+
+* Good: an order whose cancel went unanswered is canceled by the next cancel-all.
+* Bad: an order whose cancel is still in flight gets a second cancel, one more HTTP call; whether Alpaca
+  answers it 204 or 422 is not on a page read (UNVERIFIED), and both are handled.
+
+### Confirmation
+
+Offline: `test_cancel_all_cancels_again_an_order_whose_cancel_had_no_answer` in
+`packages/canon-alpaca/tests/test_execution_client.py`: a cancel that raised a read timeout, then a
+cancel-all; two cancels reach Alpaca and the order ends `CANCELED`. It failed before the change, one
+cancel sent (run 2026-11-11). Not run on paper.
+
+## Evidence
+
+Read 2026-11-11, in this checkout only; nothing fetched.
+
+* `docs/adr/0036-alpaca-adapter-order-management.md`: the skip and its reason, and the 422 path that
+  generates nothing when Alpaca shows the order canceled or pending cancel (read).
+* `packages/canon-alpaca/src/canon_alpaca/execution.py`: `_cancel`, `_cancel_refused`, and the header line
+  on `OrderPendingCancel` (read).
+* https://docs.alpaca.markets/us/reference/deleteorderbyorderid-1.md: 422 "if the order is no longer
+  cancelable", else 204, as quoted in `execution.py`; not fetched this session (UNVERIFIED).
+* `crates/adapters/{kraken,binance,bybit,interactive_brokers}/` at the pin `d53849b216e2`: cancel-all
+  including every open order, as the test's comment states; the installed wheel holds no adapter source,
+  and the network was not used (UNVERIFIED).
